      "thinking": "The ClientDisconnected errors seem unrelated to the latency spike itself. What stands out is request volume jumping from 1200 to 5400 around 07:13, with latency spiking at 07:18-07:31 as cpu_used_cores climbed to the 3.0 limit -- and cpu_limit_cores oscillating between 3.0 and 6.0 every few minutes points straight to an autoscaling pathology.\n\nI'm tracing the exact toggle pattern minute by minute (3.0, 6.0, 3.0, 6.0...) to confirm the controller is scaling up, sensing relief, then scaling back down repeatedly rather than settling, while demand itself climbed steadily and then plateaued at 5400.\n\ncpu_used_cores also climbs toward 3.0-3.5, sometimes exceeding the 3.0 limit outright (3.185, 3.307, 3.552), which plainly causes the latency spikes. This confirms the autoscaling-pathology story: the limit keeps shifting every few cycles, leaving the service under-provisioned during the \"3.0\" troughs exactly when demand needs more. Both hypotheses look identical at the bottom of each cycle, but the fact that cpu_limit itself takes multiple values is the distinguishing evidence.\n\nThere's no deploy or config change recorded, consistent with autoscaling-pathology since it needs no code change, just a controller flapping. Rechecking the metrics, the latency spikes line up tightly with the trough minutes (cpu_limit=3.0) right after a 6.0 minute -- 07:18, 07:19, 07:21, 07:24 all show rising p50/p95/p99 during these low points, confirming autoscaling-pathology as the cause.\n\nThe ClientDisconnected errors in the logs are likely just users timing out due to slow responses, not a separate root cause -- they fit the latency-driven picture rather than contradicting it. I'll finalize on autoscaling-pathology with confidence around 0.8, noting demand-saturation as a secondary possibility given the traffic growth, but the flapping cpu_limit_cores pattern is the decisive evidence favoring autoscaling-pathology.\n\n",
